{
  "_comment": [
    "The tiered-dirs parity fixture. One contract, two readers, one file: the Rust",
    "crate's tests/parity.rs at packages/rust/tiered-dirs and the TypeScript",
    "package's tests/parity.test.ts at packages/ts/tiered-dirs, which reads this",
    "file by its path in the repository. Each twin resolves every case below",
    "with its base directories stubbed and must produce `relative` byte for",
    "byte.",
    "",
    "Neither twin may carry a path shape the other lacks. When either twin gains a",
    "shape, add the case here first, watch the other twin's suite fail, then",
    "implement it there. Both suites also assert coverage —",
    "every tier x home pair and every tier x shape pair — so a new axis value with",
    "no case fails immediately rather than going untested.",
    "",
    "`vendor` is the vendor segment the suites build with, and `env_prefix` names",
    "the override variables (`<prefix>_ROOT`, `<prefix>_<HOME>_DIR`, ...).",
    "",
    "`tier` is `vendor`, `suite` or `standalone`, and `key` carries the caller's",
    "argument (null for `vendor`, which takes none). A `standalone` segment is the",
    "bare tool name with NO vendor prefix: that tier is for a general tool shipped",
    "into other people's repositories, which must not write under the vendor's",
    "namespace. Both suites assert that separately.",
    "",
    "`relative` is always \"/\"-separated, below the home's base directory, and is",
    "the only value parity pins: the base directories themselves are per-OS and",
    "are covered by each twin's own suite.",
    "",
    "The four shapes a case can take, derived from the null-ness of the fields:",
    "plain (no scope, no app), app, scope, scope+app.",
    "",
    "`scratch_cases` drive the untiered scratch root — the platform temp",
    "directory, for state that does not outlive a single call. It has no tier and",
    "no home, so it has its own list, and `relative` there is measured below the",
    "scratch root.",
    "",
    "`env_cases` drive base-directory resolution from the override variables:",
    "`BaseDirs::from_env_with` in Rust, `systemBaseDirs(vars, { env,",
    "convention })` in TS. `convention` is the constructor's convention, `native`",
    "when omitted. `env` is the variables set: the override variables,",
    "`<prefix>_CONVENTION` and `XDG_*`. `expected` pins absolute paths, which must",
    "match on both twins. A base directory it omits must equal the resolution",
    "under the default convention with no override variable set",
    "(`<prefix>_CONVENTION` and `XDG_*` kept). A row with `windows_native: true`",
    "expects XDG paths. On a Windows host, where Xdg means Native, its `expected`",
    "is dropped, `<prefix>_CONVENTION` and `XDG_*` are dropped from that",
    "resolution too, and the whole row must equal it: Xdg is Native there and",
    "`XDG_*` is ignored.",
    "The two `<prefix>_CONVENTION` precedence rows only discriminate on macOS.",
    "",
    "`convention_cases` drive the XDG layout under one home, with no platform in",
    "the way: `BaseDirs::xdg(home, var)` in Rust, `xdgBaseDirs(home, env)` in TS.",
    "`env` holds only `XDG_*` variables. `expected` pins every home; scratch is",
    "left out. A relative `XDG_*` value is ignored, as the XDG spec says.",
    "",
    "`rejected` are segments every position must refuse, the vendor included.",
    "`state`, `logs` and `run` are refused in any case: the crate puts those",
    "folders under another home's base on some OS, so a tier with one of those",
    "names could land on that home's root.",
    "",
    "`rejected_conventions` are values `<prefix>_CONVENTION` must refuse. The rule:",
    "trim, then exactly `native` or `xdg`, in lower case."
  ],
  "vendor": "com.example",
  "env_prefix": "EXAMPLE",
